# Append-only archive logs: take BOTH sides on a merge instead of conflicting.
#
# WHY: these files are written independently by two producers — the daily
# GitHub Action at 11:00 UTC and a local ./forecast/run.sh afterwards. Both
# append rows for the same snapshot date, so git sees two different additions
# at the same place and stops with a conflict. That happened on 2026-08-20 and
# blocked the push entirely.
#
# `merge=union` keeps every line from both sides rather than asking a human to
# arbitrate a log file. The duplicate rows that union merging produces are then
# collapsed on the next run: append_raw_manifest() keeps exactly one row per
# (date, source, artifact) and sets its hash to the bytes actually on disk.
# So the pair is self-healing — union never loses data, and the next capture
# reconciles it against reality.
#
# NOTE the ordering guarantee this gives up: union merge may interleave rows
# from the two sides. Nothing here depends on row order — every consumer reads
# these by key — but do not add one.
forecast/data/*/raw_manifest.csv merge=union
forecast/data/*/manifest.csv     merge=union

# Generated payloads (assets/forecast_*.json, forecast/data/*/site.json) get NO
# rule here, deliberately.
#
# The obvious thing to write is `merge=ours`, and it does not work: only text,
# binary and union are built-in low-level drivers. `ours` needs a
# merge.ours.driver config entry on every clone including the CI runner, and
# without it git silently falls back to a normal merge and conflicts anyway.
# Verified rather than assumed — it conflicted in a scratch repo.
#
# These files are rebuilt deterministically from derived/ by publish.py, so the
# resolution never needs a human reading a diff:
#
#     git checkout --ours assets/forecast_2026.json
#     python3 forecast/collect/publish.py
#     git add assets/forecast_2026.json
